  "py-run": {
    title: "The machine reads top to bottom. A name has to exist first.",
    why: "So a “name does not exist yet” error is not read as a broken machine.",
    prior: "Excel recomputes a cell. Python does the lines you wrote, in order.",
    see: "A program is lines. A lower line can see what an upper line created. Indentation is the structure, not decoration. Using form before the line form = 80 stops the machine, because form does not exist yet.",
    plain: "Read top to bottom. Fix the line. Don't start from the middle.",
    situation: "Illustration: line 1 sets n = 80. Line 2 prints n. Swap them and you get a NameError. The machine does not look ahead.",
    use: ["Run a short piece before you join it.", "The error names a line. Read that line and the one just above.", "A bad indent is a different block, not a style choice."],
    precise: "Python runs in order. A name is created when its assignment line finishes. Use it earlier and you get a NameError. Indenting in means you are inside an if, a loop, or a function. Indenting out means that block has ended. The machine does not guess the indent you “meant”.",
    insight: "The machine does not read your intention. It reads the line.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "py-type": {
    title: "“3” plus “4” is “34”. 3 plus 4 is 7.",
    why: "So a channel name is not something you take a mean of.",
    prior: "The machine runs top to bottom. Each name carries a type, and the type decides which operations are allowed.",
    see: "Integer, float, text, true/false, and None are different types. None is “nothing yet”. It is not 0 and not an empty text. The mean of a channel-name column does not exist. That is not a bad number. That is the wrong type.",
    plain: "Look at the type before you do the operation.",
    situation: "Illustration: form = \"80\" is text. form = 80 is a number. int(\"80\") becomes a number. int(\"80 form\") stops.",
    use: ["Ask the type of the name when an operation looks strange.", "Don't take the mean of a text column.", "None has to be handled on its own. Don't add it as 0."],
    precise: "int is an integer, float is a real number, str is text, bool is True or False, NoneType is None. + on two texts joins them. + on text and a number is an error, not an approximate result. A cast is an explicit line. A column that mixes text and numbers does not have a mean.",
    insight: "The wrong type does not need a prettier number.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "py-op": {
    title: "One equals assigns. Two equals compare.",
    why: "So a line that assigns is not mistaken for a true-or-false question.",
    prior: "You have types. An operator is what you do to a type.",
    see: "= stores a value in a name. == asks whether the two sides are equal. / divides and gives a float. // divides and keeps the integer part. 0.1 + 0.2 is not exactly 0.3 in machine floats. Don't use == to compare two floats you just computed.",
    plain: "Assigning and comparing are two jobs. Division comes in two kinds.",
    situation: "Illustration: the rate 80/2000 is 0.04. 80//2000 is 0. Don't take the second one and say forms are 0.",
    use: ["One = only when you mean to store.", "Compare two floats by a gap, not by ==.", "and, or, not work on true/false, not on a sentence."],
    precise: "==, !=, <, > return a bool. and stops early: if the left side is false, the right side does not run. or stops when the left side is true. not flips a bool. / in Python 3 always returns a float, including 8/2 as 4.0. // floors toward negative infinity. It is not “cut the decimals” in the everyday sense.",
    insight: "Mixing = and == is two different jobs, not a small typo.",
    symbols: [],
  },
