  "py-if": {
    title: "Only one branch runs.",
    why: "So two branches don't both change the same number.",
    prior: "You can compare two values. if is where the machine picks one block of lines.",
    see: "if asks a bool. True runs the indented block. elif is a later question, only if the earlier one was false. else is whatever remains. A non-empty text is true. 0 and an empty text are false. Don't rely on that to catch a blank table cell unless you checked.",
    plain: "Write the narrow branch first. Don't let two branches both act.",
    situation: "Illustration: form < 3 is low, elif form < 6 is mid, else high. form = 2 goes to low and does not also go to mid.",
    use: ["One branch per possibility.", "else is “whatever is left”, including what you did not think of.", "Don't nest if until it cannot be read. Split a function."],
    precise: "Branches exclude each other in the order written. Change the order and you change the result, the same way CASE works in SQL. A name assigned in one branch may not exist in the other. Using it after the if with no else is a NameError waiting.",
    insight: "Two branches both true means you have not excluded yet.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "py-loop": {
    title: "for walks a whole collection. while might not stop.",
    why: "So a loop that edits the list it is walking does not skip an item.",
    prior: "if picks one branch once. A loop repeats a block for each item.",
    see: "for name in collection: once per item. while condition: again until the condition is false. Deleting an item inside the list you are walking makes the machine skip. One item is never visited.",
    plain: "for when you know the collection. while only when you can write an exit.",
    situation: "Illustration: three channels, for prints three lines. A while with nothing that changes the condition hangs the page.",
    use: ["for over the list. Don't count by hand unless you need the position.", "If you need both the index and the item, use enumerate. It starts at 0.", "Don't add or remove items from the list you are walking."],
    precise: "range(n) is 0 through n-1, not including n. A nested loop runs the inner loop for each outer step, so a large table becomes a lot of steps. break leaves the innermost loop. continue jumps to the next item. Neither fixes the mistake of editing a list while walking it.",
    insight: "A while with no exit is not a loop. It is a hung page.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "py-fn": {
    title: "The caller receives what the function returns. No return means None.",
    why: "So a function that “already calculated” is not used when it returned nothing.",
    prior: "A loop repeats a block. A function names a block so you can call it, with clear inputs.",
    see: "def names it. Parameters are the inputs of that call. return sends a value back to the caller. If the function ends without return, the value is None. Printing 80 is not returning 80. The caller receives None, and adding None breaks.",
    plain: "If you want to use the result, return it. print is only for the eye.",
    situation: "Illustration: def rate(form, click): return form / click. rate(80, 2000) is 0.04. Forget return and the result is None. The numbers illustrate.",
    use: ["Write inputs as parameters. Don't grab an outside name because it is handy.", "If every branch needs a result, every branch returns.", "One function does one job. A second job is a second function."],
    precise: "Parameter names are local. Assigning to them does not change the outside name, unless you mutate a list that was passed in. That is a different fact: a list is an object, rebinding a name is a new name. One return leaves the function. Lines under return do not run.",
    insight: "print is not return.",
    symbols: [],
  },
