  "py-str": {
    title: "A string method returns a new string. The old one does not change itself.",
    why: "So one .strip() is not mistaken for a column that was already fixed.",
    prior: "Functions return. Text is a type, and operations on text usually return a new one.",
    see: "A str is an ordered sequence of characters. A slice [start:end] takes from start and does not take end. A method does not change the original text unless you keep the result. name.strip() without assigning it back leaves the space on name. The column is still dirty.",
    plain: "Assign it back if you want to keep the cleaned version.",
    situation: "Illustration: \" tiktok \".strip() gives \"tiktok\". The old name does not change until you assign. \"tiktok\"[0:3] is \"tik\".",
    use: ["If you want the clean version, assign: name = name.strip().", "If the job ignores case, make the case agree before you compare.", "Don't add a number onto text. Cast on purpose."],
    precise: "Indexes start at 0. A negative index counts from the end. -1 is the last character. len is the number of characters, not the number of words. An f-string is only a way to drop a value into a new text. It does not compute a wrong rate for you.",
    insight: "Calling strip and not keeping the result has changed nothing.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "py-list": {
    title: "A list is ordered, changeable, and indexed from 0.",
    why: "So the first item is not called 1.",
    prior: "A string is a sequence you don't change in place. A list is a sequence you can change.",
    see: "A list keeps order and lets you change a slot. Index 0 is the first item. -1 is the last. A slice [a:b] is a new list and does not include b. form[1] on [80, 90, 70] is 90, not 80.",
    plain: "Count from 0. A slice is a new list, not just “a look”.",
    situation: "Illustration: week = [80, 90, 70]. week[0] is 80. week.append(60) adds at the end. Don't take the mean of a list of channel names.",
    use: ["The first slot is 0 when you hand an index to someone else.", "If you need a version that doesn't touch the original, slice or copy.", "A nested list is lists inside a list, not a table with column names."],
    precise: "append adds one item. extend adds each item of a collection. append a whole list and you get one item that is that list. Assigning a new name = the old name does not copy. Both names are one list. Edit through one and the other sees it.",
    insight: "Two names can be one list.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "py-dict": {
    title: "A missing key is an error. get is what tolerates a miss.",
    why: "So a new channel does not crash the loop just because the key is new.",
    prior: "A list fetches by position. A dict fetches by key.",
    see: "A dict maps a key to a value. Each key once. form[\"tiktok\"] with no such key is a KeyError. form.get(\"tiktok\") returns None, or a value you supplied. A channel showing up halfway is normal. Treating it as a program error kills the loop exactly where the data is new.",
    plain: "Use get when missing is a possibility. Use brackets when missing is an incident.",
    situation: "Illustration: {\"gdn\": 80, \"tiktok\": 40}. Brackets on \"fb\" stop. get(\"fb\", 0) returns 0, and you have to accept that 0 means “not seen”, not “measured as 0”.",
    use: ["The key is the stable thing: a code, a name you already normalized.", "A missing key is a branch. Don't let it become a crash.", "Don't use a list as a key."],
    precise: "A key has to be unchangeable. Text, numbers, and tuples of unchangeable things are fine. A list is not. Assigning to a key that exists replaces the old value. It does not add a second row. Walking a dict walks the keys, in insertion order on current Python, but don't treat that order as a ranking.",
    insight: "A 0 that get returned is not yet a measurement.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "py-tup": {
    title: "A tuple is ordered and does not let you change a slot.",
    why: "So a row you already closed is not overwritten slot by slot later.",
    prior: "A dict needs an unchangeable key. A tuple is an unchangeable sequence, so it can be a key. A list cannot.",
    see: "A tuple is like a list in order and index, and unlike it in that you cannot append or assign a slot. (80, \"tiktok\") is a closed row. To change the forms, make a new tuple. Don't edit the old one.",
    plain: "Use a tuple for a row you don't want edited in place.",
    situation: "Illustration: week = (80, 2000). week[0] = 90 is an error. That is the lock, not a broken machine.",
    use: ["A fixed row: use a tuple.", "If you need to add an item, that is a list, or a new tuple.", "Unpacking several names at once runs only if the count of names matches."],
    precise: "The comma makes the tuple, not the parentheses. (80) is just the number 80 in parentheses. (80,) is a one-item tuple. Unpacking a, b = (80, 2000) needs exactly two names. A tuple that holds a list still lets you edit that list. The shell doesn't change. The inside might.",
    insight: "Not being able to assign a slot is the feature.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "py-set": {
    title: "A set drops duplicates. Don't ask it for an order.",
    why: "So “distinct customers” is not read as a sorted table.",
    prior: "A list keeps duplicates and keeps order. A set keeps each value once, and does not promise an order you can rank.",
    see: "A set is a collection of uniques. Adding an item you already have does not make it longer. “Is it in the set” is fast. There is no trustworthy slot 0. Three rows with the same customer code, put into a set, leave one code. You no longer know whether that code was first or last in the file.",
    plain: "Use a set to ask “who is here”. Don't use a set to keep the week's order.",
    situation: "Illustration: {\"a\", \"b\", \"a\"} leaves two codes. The printed order is not the order you put in.",
    use: ["Count distinct people: put the codes in a set, then take the length.", "If you need order, keep a list, or sort again on purpose.", "A set's items have to be unchangeable, like a dict key."],
    precise: "| is the union, & is the intersection, - is the difference. They return a new set. A set is not a table: no columns, and no duplicate row left to look up. If you want unique and also one representative row, that is not a set. That is a way of choosing a row, and you have to write how.",
    insight: "Dropping duplicates also drops the order you were remembering.",
    symbols: [],
  },
