  "py-np": {
    title: "A NumPy array is one type and a rectangle.",
    why: "So a channel name that slips into a numeric array does not turn the whole column into text.",
    prior: "A list tolerates mixed types. A numeric array does not. That is the price of calculating a column quickly.",
    see: "An ndarray has a shape, usually (rows, columns). Every cell shares one dtype. A mean along an axis is an operation on numbers. Put a text into a numeric array and you either get an error, or the whole array becomes text and .mean() is no longer the mean of forms.",
    plain: "Only put numbers in a numeric array. Names live somewhere else.",
    situation: "Illustration: three weeks [80, 90, 70], the mean is 80. Shape (3,) is one column of length 3, not a table of 3 columns. The index still starts at 0.",
    use: ["Look at dtype and shape before you take a mean.", "axis=0 walks down the rows. axis=1 walks across the columns. Say which.", "Don't put a channel code in the same array as forms."],
    precise: "NumPy forces one type. An object array means you already lost what you wanted from a numeric array. An operation on the whole array is not a for loop, and one NaN can make the whole mean NaN if you did not choose to skip it. Shape (n, 1) and (n,) do not always share an operation. Read the shape when a dimension comes out “naturally” wrong.",
    insight: "One text in a numeric column changes the whole column, not one dirty cell.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "py-ser": {
    title: "A Series adds by label, not by the position your eye sees.",
    why: "So two columns that are “the same length” are not added across the wrong customer.",
    prior: "A numeric array adds by position. A Series is a column with labels, and the label is what lines up.",
    see: "Each row of a Series has an index. Adding two Series joins on the index. A match adds. A miss becomes missing. It does not add the row below by mistake. GDN's week and TikTok's week are both length 4, but the date labels are off by a day. Adding by position adds the wrong day.",
    plain: "Look at the index before you add two columns.",
    situation: "Illustration: days 1 through 4 added to days 2 through 5. The eye sees four numbers on four numbers. A Series only adds the days that match. The offset days stay empty.",
    use: ["Set the index to the row's identity, usually a date or a code.", "If you want to add by position, reset the index on purpose, and accept the risk.", "A NaN after an alignment is a miss, not a 0."],
    precise: "A Series carries one dtype for the values, like an array. The index can repeat. If it repeats, alignment multiplies rows. You no longer have one row per day. iloc takes by position. loc takes by label. Mix them up and you take what the eye sees but the wrong date, or the reverse.",
    insight: "The same length is not the same date.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "py-frame": {
    title: "loc is labels. iloc is positions. A loc slice includes the end.",
    why: "So a slice “through day 4” does not drop day 4 or swallow an extra row.",
    prior: "A Series is one column. A DataFrame is several columns sharing an index, which is a table.",
    see: "loc[row, column] uses labels. iloc uses positions, from 0. loc[\"a\":\"c\"] includes c. iloc[0:3] does not include position 3. Slicing labels from day 1 through day 4 with iloc, by feel, easily drops a day or takes an extra one.",
    plain: "Say whether you are holding a label or a position.",
    situation: "Illustration: a table of 5 rows, iloc[0:2] is the first two. loc from label 0 through label 2, if those are the labels, includes label 2. Three rows, not two.",
    use: ["Filter a column with a condition. Don't slice by a row number you guessed.", "A text column and a number column stay separate. Don't take the mean of the whole table.", "Split learning rows and scoring rows before any duplication of rows."],
    precise: "One row is a grain you have to name. df[\"form\"] is a Series. df[[\"form\"]] is a one-column table. The two do not share every function. loc with a label slice includes both ends, unlike a list slice and unlike iloc. Assigning into a slice sometimes touches the original and sometimes does not, depending on whether it was a view. To be sure, assign a column from a Series already aligned on the index, and read a few rows back.",
    insight: "Including the end or not including it are two different slices.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "py-plot": {
    title: "A plot is a sentence. The axis needs a unit.",
    why: "So a bar of counts is not read as a rate.",
    prior: "The table already has a grain. The plot does not get to change the question away from that grain.",
    see: "Bars compare groups. A line follows time. The axis states the unit. The title is the claim, not the name of the chart type. 200 forms and 20 forms are sizes. 4% and 8% are rates. Drawing 200 next to 20 and calling a channel better has not divided by clicks.",
    plain: "Pick the picture from the sentence, label the unit, and don't let the axis lie.",
    situation: "Illustration: GDN has 80 forms on 4,000 clicks, which is 2%. TikTok has 40 forms on 1,000 clicks, which is 4%. Bars of forms say GDN is larger. Bars of rates say the opposite. Both can be right for two different sentences. The numbers illustrate.",
    use: ["Write the sentence before you call the plot.", "Put the unit on the axis.", "Don't use a line for channels that have no time order."],
    precise: "A Matplotlib or Seaborn figure only draws what you hand it. It does not divide by the denominator for you. An axis that does not start at 0 makes a small gap look like a jump. Two units on one axis is a picture that lies, unless you label both scales very clearly. Color is not an analysis.",
    insight: "With no denominator, don't draw a race.",
    symbols: [],
  },
