  "sql-like": {
    title: "LIKE is a simple pattern. A regex is a different job.",
    why: "So a pattern “contains the text form” is not written as a regular expression each engine reads differently.",
    prior: "You can already count and group. Filtering text is another WHERE, and it is easy to confuse with a regex.",
    see: "LIKE uses % for any stretch and _ for one character. REGEXP or RLIKE is a regular expression, depending on the engine, and it is not the same library as LIKE. WHERE channel LIKE '%tok' matches tiktok. It also matches junk that ends in tok. With no anchor, don't treat it as the whole cell.",
    plain: "“Contains a piece” is LIKE. “Matches a character rule” is a regex, and you name the engine.",
    situation: "Illustration: a code shaped like F- and then two digits. LIKE 'F-__' is one F, one dash, two characters. That is not yet a regex for “exactly two digits”. On many engines the regex is written separately and may not use an index.",
    use: ["Starts with a piece: 'tok%'. Ends: '%tok'. Contains: '%tok%'.", "If you need real digits or a rule, use that engine's regex, and name the engine.", "Don't regex a date column. Use a date function."],
    precise: "An unanchored pattern matches the middle of the cell. Case depends on the collation, not on your mood. A function or a regex wrapped around a column usually means an index on that column is not used. LIKE is not a safe way to split a date that was stored as text. Cast it to a date.",
    insight: "%tok is not “exactly the channel tok”.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "sql-date": {
    title: "A date is a date. Text that looks like a date does not do calendar arithmetic.",
    why: "So “yesterday” does not become a string you slice by hand.",
    prior: "LIKE was about text. A date is its own type, and wrapping a function around a date column can drop the index.",
    see: "Store a date as a date type. YEAR, DATE, DATEDIFF do calendar work. Slicing a string does text work, and it breaks when the format changes. A missing day in the table is a missing row. It is not a row of 0 forms, unless you created that 0 row.",
    plain: "Don't wrap YEAR() on the column if you need that column's index to filter a range.",
    situation: "Illustration: filter a month with day >= '2026-11-01' and day < '2026-12-01'. This half-open window does not depend on how many days the month has. Wrapping MONTH(day) = 11 may not use the index.",
    use: ["Keep a DATE or TIMESTAMP type. Don't store text.", "Filter with a range. Don't wrap a function on the column if you can avoid it.", "A day with no row is not a 0."],
    precise: "A time zone makes a TIMESTAMP cross a date boundary when you only look at the date part. Name the column's zone. DATEDIFF counts by the engine's rule, usually a count of date boundaries, not hours divided by 24. A string '11/02/2026' is unclear between 2 November and 11 February. Don't store text and then guess.",
    insight: "Slicing a date string is a bug waiting for the format to change.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "sql-nest": {
    title: "A subquery is not “more advanced” than a join.",
    why: "So a correlated subquery is not kept just because it looks hard.",
    prior: "You can filter dates and text. A subquery is one way to write “among the rows that satisfy another query”.",
    see: "An uncorrelated subquery runs on its own. A correlated one runs again for each outer row, because it uses that row's columns. “Customers above the mean of their own channel” is correlated. It is not automatically faster than a JOIN to a table of means.",
    plain: "Pick the query you can read. Measure if it is slow. Don't upgrade by nesting more.",
    situation: "Illustration: IN (SELECT code FROM ...) is a set of codes. A JOIN to that table is often the same idea, and the grain is easier to see. NULL inside the set makes IN and NOT IN differ. NOT IN that meets a NULL can return no rows.",
    use: ["If it does not use an outer column, the subquery runs once.", "If it uses an outer column, say it is correlated.", "NULL inside IN: don't assume NOT IN is still the complement."],
    precise: "EXISTS asks whether any row exists. It does not ask for a value. It is often kinder than IN when you don't need the column. A correct correlated subquery can still scan the inner table for every outer row. A JOIN to an aggregated table is how you see whether you are multiplying the grain. “Hard to read” is not an optimization.",
    insight: "Nesting more does not make the query righter.",
    symbols: [],
  },
