  "sql-nwin": {
    title: "Naming a window names it. The meaning does not change.",
    why: "So a WINDOW written once is not read as a new kind of calculation.",
    prior: "Lag and frame already have a meaning. A name only saves you from copying the same OVER.",
    see: "WINDOW w AS (PARTITION BY ... ORDER BY ...) and then OVER w reuses that exact window. Two functions that share a window name share the partition and the frame. One function is not “preferred”.",
    plain: "Use a name when a long OVER is repeated. Don't change the meaning with the name.",
    situation: "Illustration: a running total and a lag share PARTITION BY channel ORDER BY day. Name it once. Change the frame on one function and the other does not change, unless it uses that same name.",
    use: ["A long OVER you repeat: give it a name.", "Check which functions use that name.", "The name does not fix a week that the frame is missing."],
    precise: "A WINDOW clause is an alias of a specification. The engine does not run it into a middle table you can see. Renaming it does not change the result. Two different names are two specifications, even if they look the same. The default frame is still each function's default if you do not override it.",
    insight: "A window's name is not a new calculation.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "sql-proc": {
    title: "A procedure is a saved script. It can do work outside the question.",
    why: "So a “run the procedure” button is not mistaken for a transparent SELECT.",
    prior: "CASE put a rule in the query. A procedure puts a whole script on the server, and a person reading the SELECT does not see all of it.",
    see: "A stored procedure is a saved program. It can hold many statements and it can write tables. A function (UDF) returns a value and should not hide a table scan. A function wrapped around a column in WHERE can run per row and skip the index. The reader only sees the function's name.",
    plain: "If you only need a query that is easier to read, use a CTE. Don't promote it into a procedure.",
    situation: "Illustration: a procedure “close the week” both SELECTs and INSERTs into a closed table. Call it again and it writes more. That is not a view. A tax UDF that scans a price table on the inside does not show that price on the outer query.",
    use: ["Read only, and give it a name: a CTE or a view.", "If it writes a table, call it a procedure, and say where it writes.", "A function in WHERE: ask whether it wraps a column and whether it scans a table."],
    precise: "Permission to execute a procedure does not let the caller see every table inside. That can be what you want, and it is also how a wrong number hides. A function should be plain: same inputs, same output, no writes. A function that writes a table is a procedure wearing a function's name. Don't leave this year's tax rule in a procedure with no owner.",
    insight: "Hiding the script does not make the number righter.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "sql-cur": {
    title: "A cursor walks row by row. A query walks the whole set.",
    why: "So a loop “just to be sure” does not replace a query that can already say it as a set.",
    prior: "A procedure can already hide many statements. A cursor is the slowest kind of hiding: hold one row, do something, move on.",
    see: "A cursor opens a result and then steps through it in a program. Set-based is one statement for the whole group. Summing forms by channel with a FETCH loop is a rewrite of GROUP BY, slower, and easy to forget to CLOSE.",
    plain: "Write one statement. A cursor only when the next step is a side effect outside the query, one row at a time, and you accept the slowness.",
    situation: "Illustration: sending one email per customer who qualifies might need a row at a time, because the email is an outside effect. A total of forms does not. A total is one statement.",
    use: ["Sums, counts, filters: one statement.", "An outside effect per row: only then ask about a cursor.", "If you open it, close it. A cursor left open holds a session."],
    precise: "A cursor holds state: open, fetch, done, close. Forgetting to close it on a web session leaks. A set statement lets the engine choose how to read. A cursor forbids that. It is not “more exact” than GROUP BY. It is a programming loop sitting on SQL.",
    insight: "Walking row by row does not add more correctly.",
    symbols: [],
  },
