  "ens-oob": {
    title: "A row the bag did not hold is that tree's score. Not next month.",
    why: "So an out-of-bag score is not briefed as a week that has not happened.",
    prior: "Each tree has a bag, and some rows were left out. Those left-out rows score that tree, if they are really other people.",
    see: "Out-of-bag means the rows not drawn into a tree's bag. They can score that tree. They still belong to the learning set. They are not a future month. Duplicate the rare person before the split and one copy can sit in the bag while the other is called “out of bag”. The score looks good because it is the same person.",
    plain: "OOB only after the final scoring set is already split off. And don't call it the future.",
    situation: "Illustration: 1,000 customers in the learning set. One tree leaves out about a third. That third scores this tree. Next week is not inside those 1,000 customers. Duplicating 8 leavers before the split is how OOB starts lying.",
    use: ["Split off the final scoring set first.", "OOB is a learning row that missed that tree's bag.", "Don't duplicate before you draw the bags."],
    precise: "Each tree has a different OOB set. The average of those scores is an estimate on the learning set, usually more optimistic than a set held out by time, because the OOB rows are still from the same period as the bag. It does not replace a check through time. The same person in both the bag and the OOB breaks this estimate in exactly the way leakage does.",
    insight: "Out of the bag is not next week.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "imb-smote": {
    title: "SMOTE makes a point that sits between. It does not make a customer.",
    why: "So a synthetic point is not allowed into the scoring set.",
    prior: "Duplicating a rare row does not create a customer. Neither does SMOTE. It only places a point on the segment between two rare rows.",
    see: "SMOTE interpolates between minority rows in the space of the numeric columns. The new point is not a person who bought. Run SMOTE before the split and a point in between can stand with one foot in the learning set and one foot in the scoring set. That is leakage.",
    plain: "Split first. SMOTE only on the learning set. Don't score the fake points.",
    situation: "Illustration: two leavers, one spent 2 million, one spent 4. SMOTE can add a point at 3 million. Nobody spent 3 million. If that 3 million lands in the scoring set, the model is scored on a person who does not exist.",
    use: ["Split learning and scoring first.", "Interpolate only on the learning set.", "The scoring set keeps the real rows only."],
    precise: "Interpolation assumes the straight segment between two rare rows is still the rare class. On a text column a straight segment means nothing. Running SMOTE on the whole file and splitting afterwards is leakage, the same family as duplicating before the split. SMOTE does not fix a wrong label. It multiplies the labels you already have, including dirty ones.",
    insight: "A point in between is not a third customer.",
    symbols: [],
  },
