  "xa-text": {
    title: "A text function returns text. It does not turn text into a number.",
    why: "So the LEFT of a code is not added like money.",
    prior: "A cell in C0 already has a type. An advanced text function in Excel still returns text.",
    see: "LEFT, RIGHT, MID, TRIM return text. If you want a number, wrap an explicit cast. LEFT of “80 form” takes “80” and it is still text. Adding that column gives 0 or joins, depending on the cell.",
    plain: "Cleaning text is one step. Turning it into a number is the next step, if it is a number.",
    situation: "Illustration: code F-12. LEFT takes F, TRIM drops spaces. None of those return the number 12 unless you split and cast. Don't add the code column.",
    use: ["TRIM when there are extra spaces.", "Split the code and the number into two columns.", "A text column does not get SUM."],
    precise: "CONCAT joins text. It does not add money. VALUE, or multiplying by 1, can turn numeric text into a number, and it will fail on text that is not a number. That is what you want: a visible error, instead of a quiet total that is short. PROPER and UPPER do not make a code into the right lookup key if the other table does not share the rule.",
    insight: "Cutting a code does not produce an amount of money.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "xa-count": {
    title: "COUNT counts numbers. COUNTA counts cells that are not empty.",
    why: "So a column of names is not counted with COUNT and then reported as no customers.",
    prior: "Text functions don't yield numbers. A count also looks at the type, not at “looks like something is there”.",
    see: "COUNT counts numeric cells. COUNTA counts non-empty cells, including text. COUNTBLANK counts empty cells. A column of 100 customer names, COUNT returns 0. The customers are not gone. The function was the wrong count.",
    plain: "Names use COUNTA. Money uses COUNT. Say what you are counting.",
    situation: "Illustration: 100 names and 90 numeric form cells, 10 form cells blank. COUNT of the form column is 90. COUNTA of the name column is 100. “100 forms” would be wrong.",
    use: ["Counting people in a text column: COUNTA, after the header row is out.", "Counting measurements: COUNT.", "A blank is its own fact. Don't let COUNT turn it into “no person”."],
    precise: "COUNT skips text, blanks, and errors. It does not warn you that it skipped them. A column of text codes gives a COUNT of 0 even when the column is full. COUNTA counts a cell that holds only a space if the cell is not truly empty, depending on the contents. Look at a few cells. Don't trust the count alone.",
    insight: "A COUNT of 0 on a name column is the wrong function.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "xa-match": {
    title: "INDEX and MATCH can look left. VLOOKUP cannot.",
    why: "So you don't rearrange a whole table just because the column you need sits left of the key.",
    prior: "C0 already has VLOOKUP, #N/A, and the approximate-match trap. This session is how you look up when the direction is not to the right.",
    see: "MATCH returns the position of the key. INDEX takes the cell at that position, in the column you point at. That column can sit to the left. Channel name in column A, code in column B. VLOOKUP by code cannot fetch the name, because the name is to the left. INDEX/MATCH can.",
    plain: "If the key is not the leftmost column of what you need, don't use VLOOKUP.",
    situation: "Illustration: MATCH of a code with match_type 0 is an exact find. Not found is #N/A, the same meaning as VLOOKUP FALSE. Don't use match_type 1 if the column is not sorted.",
    use: ["MATCH with 0 when you need the exact key.", "INDEX on the answer column. It does not need to stand on the right.", "#N/A is still not found. Don't wrap it into a fake name."],
    precise: "INDEX(return_column, MATCH(key, key_column, 0)). match_type 0 is exact. 1 is approximate on an ascending column, the same trap as VLOOKUP TRUE. MATCH does not care which side the return column is on. Inserting a row above the range shifts the position if the range is not locked. That is the job of $, already covered in C0.",
    insight: "Looking left is not a trick. It is the direction of the column.",
    symbols: ["an exact key, not an approximate one"],
  },
  "xa-what": {
    title: "Changing an input cell is not seeing the future.",
    why: "So a what-if table is not carried out as a forecast that was tested.",
    prior: "A lookup fetched a number that is in the table. What-if asks “if this cell were different, what would the formula return”.",
    see: "A data table and a scenario change input cells of a formula you already wrote. They do not learn from new data. Change the price from 100 to 120 and the profit on the file moves by the formula. Next week's price might not be 120.",
    plain: "Call it a test of the formula. Don't call it a forecast.",
    situation: "Illustration: profit = units × price − cost. A two-way table changes units and price. Each cell is one recomputation of that formula, not a week that happened.",
    use: ["Write the formula and which cells are inputs.", "Read the table as situations of the formula.", "If you want to talk about next week, that is a forecast, in C4, not a data table."],
    precise: "Goal Seek flips an input so an output hits a number. It is still your formula, and several inputs can hit one target. Scenario Manager is only saved sets of inputs. None of them is the truth until that job happens and you measure it again.",
    insight: "An assumed cell is not an observation.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "xa-macro": {
    title: "A macro repeats clicks. It does not decide.",
    why: "So a macro button is not given the job “pick the channel for me”.",
    prior: "What-if was only a formula. A macro is a recording of clicks, including a foolish click.",
    see: "A macro reruns a recorded sequence. Change the file's structure and the sequence clicks the wrong place. Record a macro while the wrong channel is filtered and next time it filters the wrong one, very consistently.",
    plain: "A macro is for a dull repeated job. The decision stays outside the macro.",
    situation: "Illustration: every morning, paste numbers into a fixed range. A macro can do that. “Do we raise the budget” is not in the macro, because no click contains that sentence.",
    use: ["Only record steps that were already right on a sample file.", "If the columns change, edit the macro. Don't hope it understands.", "Don't put a business decision inside a click."],
    precise: "A macro is code, usually VBA, even if you recorded it with a button. It can change data without asking again. A file with a macro is a file with a program. Opening one from a stranger runs their program. A macro does not add a test, does not add a model, and does not know the grain of the table.",
    insight: "Steady is not the same as right. A macro is only steady.",
    symbols: [],
  },
