  "sa-tune": {
    title: "Fine-tune changes the weights. RAG changes the documents in the prompt.",
    why: "So tomorrow's price table is not handed to a fine-tune from today.",
    prior: "The three layers already split model and data. Fine-tune touches the model. RAG touches the data of the call.",
    see: "Fine-tune adjusts weights with training examples. RAG leaves the weights and puts document passages into the prompt before writing. The price changes every morning. Last night's fine-tune does not know this morning's price. RAG only knows if this morning's price passage is put into the call.",
    plain: "Documents that change often: RAG. A stable voice, and the facts rarely change: only then consider a fine-tune.",
    situation: "Illustration: the return policy changes one sentence. The job is for the new sentence to sit in the document that gets retrieved. Retraining the whole model for one sentence is the wrong layer.",
    use: ["A fact that changes by the day: don't fine-tune to memorize that fact.", "RAG is only right if the retrieved passage is the one that is in force.", "A fine-tune does not replace a database."],
    precise: "A fine-tune does not guarantee the model memorized a table you can look up like a primary key. It changes how it writes. RAG does not make the model “understand” the documents. It only lets this call see a few passages. Neither is a transaction of the price. The price should stay in a table, and a tool should read the table if the product needs a correct number.",
    insight: "If the price changes tomorrow, training last night is not the plan.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "sa-react": {
    title: "Think, call a tool, look at the result. Don't invent the tool's result.",
    why: "So a sentence “I looked at the catalog” does not count if there was no catalog call.",
    prior: "Fine-tune and RAG were two ways to change what the model sees. ReAct is a loop: write a step, call a tool, read what the tool returns.",
    see: "ReAct is reason, then act with a tool, then observe exactly what the tool returned. The observation is not a sentence the model guesses. With no log line that called the catalog, you may not state the stock. Even if the sentence is very sure.",
    plain: "Every fact from a system needs one call and one result.",
    situation: "Illustration: the question is whether it is in stock. The act step calls the catalog by code. The observation is the number the catalog returns. Without that step the answer must say it has not looked, and it may not invent “3 left”.",
    use: ["The log needs the tool name and the result.", "A strange result: return that result. Don't edit it to look nice.", "When the step cap is hit, stop and say it is not finished."],
    precise: "This loop can repeat. With no cap on steps it spends tokens and never answers. The cap is a product limit, the same family as a constraint you cannot spend twice. If the tool returns an error, the observation is that error, not a pretty result the model fills in.",
    insight: "No log means it has not looked.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "sa-stage": {
    title: "Three stages: talk from the prompt, call the catalog, then score cases. Don't invent a transcript.",
    why: "So a ShopAssist assignment is not replaced by an invented script.",
    prior: "ReAct was the tool-calling loop. ShopAssist in the syllabus walks three stages. There is no sample transcript to memorize.",
    see: "Stage one is talking from the prompt. Stage two is the tool and the catalog. Stage three is scoring cases you already have. The notebook does not invent the lines of a call. A line “the customer said: give me the 2-million package” is not in the material, so don't add it to make the case easier to score.",
    plain: "Keep the three stages. A missing log for a stage means that stage has not happened.",
    situation: "Illustration: stage one has no catalog, so it may not state a price. Stage two is where the catalog result exists. Stage three compares cases to a measure you wrote beforehand, not to an invented transcript.",
    use: ["Stage one: only what is in the prompt.", "Stage two: every stock or price number comes from a tool log.", "Stage three: score the cases you wrote. Don't add cases to make it look good."],
    precise: "The syllabus introduces three stages. None of them hands you a canonical transcript. Writing a conversation and then scoring that conversation is scoring something you just invented. The cases in stage three have to be written beforehand, with the correct result taken from the catalog or the policy, not from a fluent model reply.",
    insight: "There is no sample transcript. Don't manufacture one.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "sa-tool": {
    title: "Price and stock come from the catalog. Not from a fluent sentence.",
    why: "So stage two is not written as a paragraph with no product code.",
    prior: "The three stages already have a place for the catalog. This stage is where a number is allowed to appear, and only from the tool.",
    see: "The catalog tool takes a code and returns the fields that exist: price, stock, conditions. No code, no number. The model writes “about 2 million” while the catalog was not called. That is not an estimate. That is an invented number.",
    plain: "With no catalog line, there is no price in the answer.",
    situation: "Illustration: code A12 returns a price and a stock. The answer repeats those fields. If there is no code A12 and the customer asks for a package with no code, the answer is that the code was not found, not an approximate price.",
    use: ["Call with a code, not with an adjective.", "Return the fields the catalog returned.", "Code not found: say not found. Don't interpolate a price."],
    precise: "A catalog is a system, the same law as a SQL table: no row means no number, not a number 0. A tool error returns an error. Wrapping the error as “temporarily out of stock” changes the meaning. A refund policy is also a document, not a price. Don't take the price from the policy if the catalog is the source of price.",
    insight: "No code, no price.",
    symbols: [],
  },
  "sa-score": {
    title: "Score the cases you wrote. Several similar replies are not the truth.",
    why: "So self-consistency is not read as the catalog having been checked.",
    prior: "The catalog is already the source of numbers. Stage three asks whether the cases stick to that source, not whether the model sounds sure.",
    see: "Self-consistency is several runs, then the sentence that shows up most. If the runs invent the same price, the frequent sentence is still an invented price. Five runs all say “in stock” while the catalog says none left. The agreement is five wrong runs.",
    plain: "The measure of a case is the catalog result or the sentence in the policy. Not how often the model repeats itself.",
    situation: "Illustration: a case written beforehand: code A12, the catalog returns out of stock. The model says in stock. That case is wrong, however sure it sounds. Don't add a new case to fill the score.",
    use: ["Cases are written beforehand, with the correct result from the source.", "Score each case. Don't score smoothness.", "Several runs agreeing: that is only agreement. Still check the source."],
    precise: "Self-consistency reduces one kind of noise in how tokens are chosen. It does not create a source. A case with no correct result from the catalog or from the policy passage cannot be scored, and you also may not invent a correct result so that you can. Don't manufacture a transcript to get more cases.",
    insight: "Agreeing with each other is not agreeing with the catalog.",
    symbols: [],
  },
